  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Four students stole the coronation stone and snapped it in two. Christmas morning, 1950. Westminster Abbey. A hundred and fifty kilos of sandstone.",
        "visual_query": "westminster abbey gothic stone interior",
        "visual_fallback": "old stone cathedral"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scottish kings were crowned on it until Edward the First seized it in 1296. After that, English and British monarchs were crowned over it.",
        "visual_query": "medieval scottish castle hillside",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient carved stone"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They drove it north in pieces. Police hunted it for months. Then it turned up on an abbey altar in Arbroath.",
        "visual_query": "arbroath abbey ruins scotland",
        "visual_fallback": "ruined abbey arches"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So what happened to the four thieves? No charges. They were traced within months, and the Crown simply chose not to prosecute.",
        "visual_query": "scottish highland road winter",
        "visual_fallback": "empty country road"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1996 Britain handed the stone back to Scotland, on condition it returns to London for every coronation. It now sits in a museum in Perth.",
        "visual_query": "perth scotland river tay town",
        "visual_fallback": "stone museum display"
      }
    ],
